.TH EZCONFIG 1 "November 2026" "ezconfig 0.26.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.SH NAME
ezconfig \- read and change INI configs, with an optional schema
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ezconfig
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR CHAR ]
.RI [ COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ezconfig
is the single source of truth for a config: one tool that reads and
changes it, for people and for programs.
.PP
Without a command it opens an interactive shell with tab completion.
Changes stay in memory until saved; an asterisk in the prompt
.RB ( *> )
means something is unsaved, and
.B changes
lists it.
.PP
With a command it runs that one command and exits. These one-shot
commands are meant for programs and scripts: data goes to standard
output, messages to standard error, and the exit status says what
happened. Writes are checked first and saved atomically.
.B ask
is the one command meant for people.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE"
The config to use. Default:
.BR config.ini .
.TP
.BI \-s " FILE"
The schema to use, or
.B none
to read the config exactly as written. Default:
.B schema.ini
next to the config, if there is one.
.TP
.BI \-d " CHAR"
The delimiter between section and key in an address (one-shot commands
only; in the shell, use
.BR delim ).
Default: a period
.RB ( . ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
These work both on the command line and in the shell.
.TP
.BI get " KEY..."
Print values, one per line. All or nothing: if any key is missing,
nothing is printed.
.TP
.BI set " KEY=VALUE..."
Set values. On the command line they are checked against the schema and
saved; in the shell they wait for
.BR save .
.B set
never asks for a value; use
.BR ask .
.TP
.BI unset " KEY..."
Remove keys. With a schema, a key with a default falls back to it.
.TP
.BR keys " [\fISECTION\fR]"
List key addresses, for everything or one section.
.TP
.B sections
List section names.
.TP
.BR list " [\fISECTION\fR]"
Print
.I KEY=VALUE
lines, for everything or one section.
.TP
.BR emit " [\fISECTION\fR]"
Print everything, or one section, as JSON for programs: root keys at the
top level, each section an object, every value a string.
.TP
.B check
Report problems: values that break the schema, required keys with no
value, root keys that share their name with a section, and section names
containing
.B =
or brackets (other INI readers accept them, but ezconfig never creates
them).
.TP
.BR explain " [\fINAME\fR...]"
Explain keys and sections from the schema, or everything.
.TP
.BR ask " [\fINAME\fR...]"
Ask for values, one question at a time: everything the schema covers, or
the sections and keys named. Each question shows what the value should
look like, with the current value in brackets; Enter keeps it, Tab
offers the choices, and the up arrow recalls the current value and the
default. Needs a terminal.
.IP
On the command line,
.B ask
completes the change: it ends by listing the changes and asking: save,
redo, or quit. That question has no default, so pressing Enter once too
often can't lose or save anything. In the shell,
.B ask
is one tool among the others: the answers join the session's unsaved
changes, one line says what changed, and
.BR changes ,
.B save
and
.B revert
do the rest.
.TP
.B help
Show the commands.
.TP
.B version
Show the version.
.SH SHELL COMMANDS
These work only in the shell.
.TP
.BI wipe " SECTION..."
Remove whole sections (asks first).
.TP
.BI copy " SRC DEST"
Copy a section to a new one.
.TP
.BI move " SRC DEST"
Move or rename a section.
.TP
.BR changes " [\fISECTION\fR]"
Show unsaved changes:
.B ~
changed,
.B +
added,
.B \-
removed.
.TP
.B status
Show the config and schema, the file's state and unsaved changes, what
it holds and how many problems it has, and session settings.
.TP
.B path
Show the config's full path.
.TP
.BR save " [\fIFILE\fR]"
Write the changes, or write a copy to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B revert
Discard unsaved changes and reload.
.TP
.BR history " [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR]"
Show or switch command history for this config.
.TP
.BR delim " [\fICHAR\fR]"
Show or change the delimiter.
.TP
.B quit
Leave (asks first if anything is unsaved).
.SH ADDRESSING
A key is
.I name
(a root key, before any section) or
.IR section.name .
The delimiter alone,
.BR . ,
means the root keys. Where a command takes a
.IR NAME ,
a bare name is the root key, and also the section of that name if there
is one;
.I name.\&
with the delimiter after it is only the section.
.SH SCHEMA
A schema is itself an INI file. Its ordinary sections and root keys are
defaults, shaped like the config. Blocks whose names start with
.B @
describe the config:
.TP
.BI [@rule " ADDRESS" ]
One key: its
.B type
(string, int, float, bool, enum, or a built-in type),
.BR min ,
.BR max ,
.BR values ,
.BR pattern ,
.BR required ,
and for people
.BR format ,
.B doc
and
.BR prompt .
.TP
.BI [@section " NAME" ]
A section's
.BR title ,
.B doc
and
.B keys
(the order to present them).
.B [@section .]\&
is the root keys.
.TP
.B [@ask]
What
.B ask
shows first and walks through:
.BR title ,
.BR doc ,
.BR sections ,
and what the schema is:
.B application
and
.BR version .
.PP
Built-in types: x11color, hexcolor, date, time, time12, duration, size,
ipv4, ip, host, url, email, timezone, port. Each checks the value
properly and stores it in a standard spelling.
.PP
With a schema, reading commands show what programs should see: defaults
for keys the config doesn't set, and only valid values. An invalid value
is replaced by its default, or left out, with a warning on standard
error.
.B \-s none
shows the config as written.
.SH USING FROM PROGRAMS
A program reads its settings with one call:
.PP
.RS
.B ezconfig emit
.RE
.PP
The output is the whole config as a JSON object, ready for any JSON
library: root keys at the top level, each section an object of its own.
With a schema it is already what the program should use: defaults fill
in keys the config doesn't set, values that break a rule are left out
(or replaced by their default), and bools, enums and built-in types
arrive in their standard spelling, so
.B YES
arrives as
.BR true .
The program never sees a value the schema would refuse.
.PP
Every value is a string, as in the config itself; convert the ones you
need. Because each value has already been checked, the conversion
can't fail. Decode into a plain map (root keys are strings, sections are
maps of strings) rather than a typed structure.
.PP
Check the exit status: 0 means the JSON is complete; 7 means it is
complete but a root key was renamed (see EXIT STATUS); anything else
means stop. Warnings go to standard error, never into the JSON.
.B emit
.I SECTION
gives one section on its own. A program that doesn't run in the
config's folder names it:
.BR "ezconfig \-c " "\fIC:\eSignLoop\econfig.ini\fR " emit .
.PP
PowerShell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$c = ezconfig emit | ConvertFrom\-Json
$port = [int]$c.net.port
.fi
.RE
.PP
Python:
.PP
.RS
.nf
out = subprocess.run(["ezconfig", "emit"],
                     capture_output=True, text=True)
if out.returncode not in (0, 7):
    raise SystemExit(out.stderr)
c = json.loads(out.stdout)
port = int(c["net"]["port"])
.fi
.RE
.PP
Go:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cmd := exec.Command("ezconfig", "emit")
out, _ := cmd.Output()
if code := cmd.ProcessState.ExitCode(); code != 0 && code != 7 {
    log.Fatal("ezconfig emit failed")
}
var c map[string]any
json.Unmarshal(out, &c)
net := c["net"].(map[string]any)
port, _ := strconv.Atoi(net["port"].(string))
.fi
.RE
.PP
For one or two values in a shell script,
.B get
is simpler: it prints just the values, one per line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A value breaks the schema (or
.B check
found a problem).
.TP
.B 2
Syntax error in the config or schema, or
.B emit
refused a config it can't represent as JSON (a renamed root key would
take a section's name).
.TP
.B 3
Config or schema missing or unreadable.
.TP
.B 4
Bad arguments.
.TP
.B 5
Key or section not found.
.TP
.B 6
.B ask
stopped or quit; nothing saved.
.TP
.B 7
.B emit
renamed a root key that shares its name with a section. The output is
complete; the key is shown as
.IR NAME= ,
which no INI key can contain.
.SH COLOR
In the shell and in
.BR ask ,
a little colour marks what the text already says, as git does: added
lines green, removed lines red and changed lines yellow; errors red;
notes and warnings yellow; section headings bold. Colour is used only
when the output is a terminal, so anything piped or redirected is plain,
and one-shot commands that print data never use it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set (to anything), no colour is used. A convention shared by many
tools.
.TP
.B TERM
When it is
.BR dumb ,
no colour is used.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B config.ini
The default config.
.TP
.B schema.ini
The schema, looked for next to the config.
.TP
.B .ezconfig/
A hidden folder next to the config holding its command history, only if
history is turned on.
.PP
Comments in a config are kept when it is saved, with the key or section
that follows them.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read a value in a script:
.PP
.RS
.B ezconfig get net.port
.RE
.PP
Set two values and save:
.PP
.RS
.B ezconfig set display.rotation=90 player.debug=no
.RE
.PP
Give a program the whole config, as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.B ezconfig emit > settings.json
.RE
.PP
Check a config before deploying it:
.PP
.RS
.B ezconfig \-c broken.ini check
.RE
.PP
Talk someone through one setting:
.PP
.RS
.B ezconfig ask schedule.showtime
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
README.md and the examples folder in the ezconfig source.
